<LegalLayout
  title="Privacy Policy — Kowork"
  description="How Kowork handles your data. The short version: Kowork is local-first and collects nothing."
  heading="Privacy Policy"
  lastUpdated="September 3, 2026"
>
  <p>
    Kowork is an open-source desktop application that lets you hand real work to
    AI agents. It is published by Grégoire Hertault ("we", "us"). This policy
    explains what happens to your data when you use the Kowork app and the
    getkowork.com website.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>The short version: Kowork collects nothing.</strong> No account, no analytics,
    no telemetry, no cookies. Your files and conversations stay on your device, and
    the app talks only to the services you choose, directly from your device.
  </p>

  <h2>The Kowork app</h2>

  <h3>Your data stays on your device</h3>
  <p>
    Kowork runs locally on your computer. Your folders, files, conversations,
    and settings are stored on your device and are never sent to us. We operate
    no servers that receive your content.
  </p>

  <h3>AI providers</h3>
  <p>
    To work, Kowork sends your requests — and the files you allow agents to read
    — to the AI provider you configure (for example Anthropic or OpenAI),
    directly from your device, using your own account or API key. That exchange
    is governed by your provider's privacy policy, which you should read. We
    never see or store this data.
  </p>

  <h3>Connectors</h3>
  <p>
    Kowork can connect to third-party services you choose (for example Google
    Calendar). When you connect a service:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      You sign in with the provider directly (OAuth 2.0) in your browser. Your
      password is never shared with Kowork.
    </li>
    <li>Sign-in tokens are stored on your device, not on our servers.</li>
    <li>
      Data is exchanged directly between your device and the provider. It does
      not pass through us.
    </li>
    <li>
      You can revoke access at any time from the provider's settings (for
      Google, at{" "}
      <a href="https://myaccount.google.com/permissions"
        >myaccount.google.com/permissions</a
      >) or by disconnecting the connector in Kowork.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    For a few connectors whose providers do not allow local sign-in redirects,
    the sign-in flow briefly passes through a Kowork-operated redirect endpoint
    (api.kowork.dev) that forwards you back to the app. It stores nothing and
    request logging is disabled on it.
  </p>

  <h3>Google user data and Limited Use</h3>
  <p>
    Kowork's use and transfer to any other app of information received from
    Google APIs adheres to the{" "}
    <a href="https://developers.google.com/terms/api-services-user-data-policy"
      >Google API Services User Data Policy</a
    >, including the Limited Use requirements.
  </p>
  <p>
    Kowork uses AI models to carry out your requests, and data from Google
    services you connect (such as Google Calendar) may be included in those
    requests. The use of raw or derived user data received from Google
    Workspace APIs adheres to the Google API Services User Data Policy,
    including the Limited Use requirements. In particular:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      Google user data is sent only from your device to the AI provider you
      configure, and only to carry out the task you asked for.
    </li>
    <li>
      Google user data is never used to develop, improve, or train
      generalized AI or machine-learning models.
    </li>
    <li>
      Google user data is never sold, used for advertising, or shared with
      anyone other than your chosen AI provider as needed for your request.
    </li>
    <li>
      No human at Kowork ever sees or reads your Google user data; we operate
      no servers that receive it.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Update checks</h3>
  <p>
    If enabled in settings, Kowork checks GitHub for new versions when it
    starts; you can also trigger a check from the menu, or turn the setting off.
    That request goes to GitHub, which processes your IP address and standard
    request data under the{" "}
    <a
      href="https://docs.github.com/en/site-policy/privacy-policies/github-general-privacy-statement"
      >GitHub Privacy Statement</a
    >.
  </p>

  <h2>The website</h2>
  <p>
    getkowork.com is a static website hosted on Cloudflare. Like any web host,
    Cloudflare processes standard technical logs (IP address, browser type, time
    of request) to deliver and secure the site, under the{" "}
    <a href="https://www.cloudflare.com/privacypolicy/"
      >Cloudflare Privacy Policy</a
    >. We run no analytics, no advertising, and no tracking on the website, and
    it sets no cookies.
  </p>
  <p>
    App downloads are served by GitHub Releases, so downloading the app is a
    request to GitHub covered by the GitHub Privacy Statement linked above.
  </p>

  <h2>What we don't collect</h2>
  <p>
    Because the app is local-first and the website is static, we do not collect
    or store your name, email address, files, conversations, prompts, connector
    data, usage statistics, or device identifiers.
  </p>

  <h2>Your rights</h2>
  <p>
    Since we collect no personal data, there is generally nothing held by us to
    access, correct, or delete: data created by the app lives on your device and
    disappears when you delete it there. If you are in the European Economic
    Area, you nevertheless keep all your rights under the GDPR, including the
    right to lodge a complaint with your supervisory authority (in France, the
    CNIL). For any privacy question or request, contact us as described below.
  </p>

  <h2>Changes to this policy</h2>
  <p>
    If this policy changes, the new version is published on this page and in the{
      " "
    }
    <a href="https://github.com/MrHertal/kowork">Kowork repository</a>.
    Significant changes are announced in the release notes.
  </p>

  <h2>Contact</h2>
  <p>
    Kowork is an open-source project published by Grégoire Hertault. For privacy
    questions, open an issue or discussion on{" "}
    <a href="https://github.com/MrHertal/kowork">GitHub</a>. To report a
    security vulnerability, use{" "}
    <a href="https://github.com/MrHertal/kowork/security/advisories/new"
      >GitHub Security Advisories</a
    >.
  </p>
</LegalLayout>
